<<<
TRUE & FALSE ETYMOLOGY.
===
^^TRUE & FALSE ETYMOLOGY^^.
>>>

<<<
farrow, litter. only, l. of any quadruped producing several young at a birth.
===
**farrow, litter.** *F.* is used of swine only, *l.* of any quadruped producing several young at a birth.
>>>

<<<
farther, further. The history
===
**farther, further.** The history
>>>

<<<
to be that further is a comparative of fore & should
===
to be that *further* is a comparative of *fore* & should
>>>

<<<
& | that farther is a newer variant of | further, no more connected with far than further is, but affected
===
& that *farther* is a newer variant of *further*, no more connected with *far* than *further* is, but affected
>>>

<<<
the fact that further, having come to be used instead of the obsolete comparative of far (farrer), seemed
===
the fact that *further*, having come to be used instead of the obsolete comparative of *far* (*farrer*), seemed
>>>

<<<
should assimilate it to far. This is
===
should assimilate it to *far*. This is
>>>

<<<
the form farther is usually preferred where the word is intended to be the comparative of far, while further is used where the notion of far is altogether absent
===
the form *farther* is usually preferred where the word is intended to be the comparative of *far*, while *further* is used where the notion of *far* is altogether absent
>>>

<<<
the preference of the majority is for further; the most
===
the preference of the majority is for *further*; the most
>>>

<<<
that farther is not common except where distance
===
that *farther* is not common except where distance
>>>

<<<
1. Comparative of far:—If you can bear your load no farther, say s0.— Ht Martineau. It was not thought safe for the ships to proceed further in the darkness.—Macaulay. 171 F. is used of swine |
===
**1.** Comparative of *far*:—*If you can bear your load no farther, say so.*—Ht Martineau. *It was not thought safe for the ships to proceed further in the darkness.*—Macaulay.
>>>

<<<
+ 2. No notion of far:—Down he sat without farther bidding.—Dickens. I now proceed to some further instances. —De Morgan.
===
+ **2.** No notion of *far*:—*Down he sat without farther bidding.*—Dickens. *I now proceed to some further instances.*—De Morgan.
>>>

<<<
3. Intermediate:— Punishment cannot act any farther than in as far as the idea of it is present in the mind.—Bentham. Men who pretend to believe no further than they can see.—Berkeley.
===
**3.** Intermediate:—*Punishment cannot act any farther than in as far as the idea of it is present in the mind.*—Bentham. *Men who pretend to believe no further than they can see.*—Berkeley.
>>>

